WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.739 --> 00:00:03.110
We are delighted to have in our studios

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:04.770
Dr. Rajendra Pachauri,

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.210
the director general

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.669
of the Energy Research Institute of

00:00:08.669 --> 00:00:11.529
India,

00:00:11.529 --> 00:00:15.209
and the chairman of the Intergovernmental

00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:18.060
Panel on Climate Change.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.210
He, along with

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:21.269
Mr. Al Gore,

00:00:21.269 --> 00:00:24.019
received the 2007

00:00:24.019 --> 00:00:26.640
Nobel Peace Prize.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.710
And he was of course

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.989
a teacher at Yale University for several years,

00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:34.160
and we're delighted to welcome him back to the
campus. Welcome.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.960
Thank you.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.060
Despite all the

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.430
news reports that we see, the newspapers,

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:43.400
television,

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.120
there is still a considerable body of

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:46.720
opinion,

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.410
which believe that this talk of global warming

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:51.200
is a big,

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.520
overblown

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.080
theory based on

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:00.570
some fragmented data, and perhaps it's only

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:01.360
a cyclical thing,

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.780
not something systemic.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.390
So what do you say to the skeptics?

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:10.710
Well let me first say that, you know, the process
by which the IPCC prepares its

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:11.880
report

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.960
is the strongest testimony to the strength
of what we produce.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.020
We mobilized the best scientists and experts
from all over the world,

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.700
and they are selected on the basis of nominations

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.470
sent by governments

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.450
and on the basis of CVs of the people

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.440
nominated.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:33.900
Just to give you an example, in the fourth assessment
report we got close to

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.810
two thousand nominations,

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:40.390
and roughly 450 were selected as those who directly
wrote the report.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:45.390
And these are scientists from different branches of science?
Every branch of science

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.869
depending on the

00:01:46.869 --> 00:01:50.910
subjects we're covering in the full assessment,

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:51.770
and

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:55.270
over and above that we have about 2,500

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:56.460
reviewers

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.470
because every draft is carefully reviewed

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:02.210
by a whole range of people,

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:02.860
experts,

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:06.140
and then at the second stage by governments.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:10.179
Each of the comments provided by the reviewers
is carefully logged,

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:15.259
and the authors decide whether to accept it or
reject it. If for some reason it is rejected,

00:02:15.259 --> 00:02:17.069
the reasons have to be recorded.

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:22.030
So you can't think of a more transparent
and open process than this.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:26.870
Also, may I emphasize that the IPCC does no research
on its own.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:31.039
It carries out its assessment on the basis
of peer reviewed literature.

00:02:31.039 --> 00:02:34.919
And there's a wealth of literature, in fact its 
growing so rapidly

00:02:34.919 --> 00:02:38.249
now unless all the people who are researching
this

00:02:38.249 --> 00:02:40.689
are basically in the business of fooling
the public,

00:02:40.689 --> 00:02:43.290
you really can't believe there's anything wrong.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.250
The second point I'd like to make is

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.159
you know the number of skeptics

00:02:47.159 --> 00:02:51.290
as would be expected has been going down very rapidly.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.869
But, on the other hand if you look at the history
of science and knowledge

00:02:54.869 --> 00:02:59.889
in any new discovery, in any new field of
human endeavor

00:02:59.889 --> 00:03:02.219
where new knowledge is created,

00:03:02.219 --> 00:03:06.439
there are skeptics who will question it for some
time to come.

00:03:06.439 --> 00:03:10.999
There are even today people who believe that
the earth is flat.

00:03:10.999 --> 00:03:13.289
So, I mean,

00:03:13.289 --> 00:03:14.129
what else can one say?

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:16.399
Tell us for our viewers

00:03:16.399 --> 00:03:20.459
in brief what the conclusion of the IPCC has been.

00:03:20.459 --> 00:03:25.209
Well, firstly let me explain how climate
change has been caused by human actions.

00:03:25.209 --> 00:03:28.169
We have been in the process of industrialization,

00:03:28.169 --> 00:03:31.659
consuming larger and larger quantities
of fossil fuels,

00:03:31.659 --> 00:03:36.269
and there are other gases also which have the
same effect as carbon dioxide,

00:03:36.269 --> 00:03:38.059
though carbon dioxide is by far

00:03:38.059 --> 00:03:41.650
the most important of these gases.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:46.279
Before industrialization the concentration
of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere

00:03:46.279 --> 00:03:48.629
was 280 parts per million.

00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:52.819
It's now in the neighborhood of 380 parts per million.

00:03:52.819 --> 00:03:56.339
And may I mention that this concentration
level

00:03:56.339 --> 00:04:00.359
was last recorded six hundred and fifty thousand
years ago,

00:04:00.359 --> 00:04:01.609
and there are very

00:04:01.609 --> 00:04:06.159
well-established scientific methods by
which we can now

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:09.219
assess what the composition of the atmosphere
was

00:04:09.219 --> 00:04:10.379
going back

00:04:10.379 --> 00:04:11.670
several thousands of, 

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:14.459
hundreds of thousands of years.

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:17.329
Now because of this concentration what happens
is

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:18.039
typically

00:04:18.039 --> 00:04:20.089
you get radiation from the sun,

00:04:20.089 --> 00:04:22.400
it falls on the surface of the earth,

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.240
and a good part of it is reradiated back

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.649
into outer space.

00:04:26.649 --> 00:04:30.490
Now that's what has kept the temperature in
a balance over a period of time, and of course

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:31.840
for natural reasons

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:35.419
there have been variations over a long period
of time.

00:04:35.419 --> 00:04:38.370
But with this high concentration of gases

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:40.360
part of what is reradiated back

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.309
comes right back to the Earth,

00:04:42.309 --> 00:04:43.669
and that causes

00:04:43.669 --> 00:04:48.440
an imbalance--Greenhouse effect.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:52.770
But it's not a smooth and linear change of temperatures only.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:56.590
As a result of interference with the entire climate system

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:58.900
what we have is for instance

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:00.460
many more floods,

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.729
we have changes in precipitation, not totally
at average levels,

00:05:04.729 --> 00:05:07.780
but also precipitation patterns, for instance

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:10.900
extreme precipitation events are on the increase.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:12.909
We have many more heat waves.

00:05:12.909 --> 00:05:14.509
We have droughts,

00:05:14.509 --> 00:05:18.389
and of course the result of all this is sea level rise.

00:05:18.389 --> 00:05:19.139
And the sea is

00:05:19.139 --> 00:05:21.719
increasing in its level

00:05:21.719 --> 00:05:24.000
both because of thermal expansion

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.909
as well as melting of the ice bodies. Now that's
something which is visible,

00:05:27.909 --> 00:05:29.419
which is measured,

00:05:29.419 --> 00:05:32.300
and people can deny it if they want to,

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:33.770
but all they have to do is

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:36.789
go to the arctic region. I've been there myself.

00:05:36.789 --> 00:05:38.740
Go to the Himalayan glaciers,

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:41.219
and you'll see the rapid rate at which the

00:05:41.219 --> 00:05:43.280
the bodies of ice on the Earth are

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:44.979
melting.

00:05:44.979 --> 00:05:49.310
Just as a matter of fact the arctic region
has been warming at twice the rate

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:51.259
of the rest of

00:05:51.259 --> 00:05:52.889
the Earth.

00:05:52.889 --> 00:05:55.360
And this naturally contributes to

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:57.399
more water going into the oceans.

00:05:57.399 --> 00:06:01.580
So this is how climate change is taking place,

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:05.229
and the only way we can stabilize this
situation

00:06:05.229 --> 00:06:07.050
is for us to cut down

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.460
on emissions of greenhouse gases

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:13.439
and allow for stabilization of the concentration
of these gases

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:16.410
in the atmosphere.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:17.519
This

00:06:17.519 --> 00:06:18.449
is 

00:06:18.449 --> 00:06:20.009
very clear as to the

00:06:20.009 --> 00:06:21.919
danger involved.

00:06:21.919 --> 00:06:24.930
How do you explain

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:26.279
the fact of the

00:06:26.279 --> 00:06:28.210
Nobel Peace Prize committee

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:29.229
chose to give

00:06:29.229 --> 00:06:30.949
IPCC, you, and

00:06:30.949 --> 00:06:32.529
Al Gore the award.

00:06:32.529 --> 00:06:36.150
What is the connection between peace and our climate?

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:41.830
Well, I would say that the Norwegian Nobel committee
have been very

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.530
far-sighted

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:44.949
in this decision,

00:06:44.949 --> 00:06:48.669
which may sound a little self-serving, but

00:06:48.669 --> 00:06:50.029
the point is

00:06:50.029 --> 00:06:53.620
the result of climate change is certainly sea level rise.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:55.689
This has inbuilt in it

00:06:55.689 --> 00:06:57.609
the element of

00:06:57.609 --> 00:06:59.759
displacement of a large population.

00:06:59.759 --> 00:07:02.039
If you look at the Maldive islands,

00:07:02.039 --> 00:07:06.599
even though it may not be fully submerged
under the water, every time there's an extreme

00:07:06.599 --> 00:07:08.099
precipitation event

00:07:08.099 --> 00:07:09.869
because of higher sea levels,

00:07:09.869 --> 00:07:12.530
the severity of the impacts is much worse

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:14.460
than would have been the case otherwise.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:16.210
If you look at the tsunami,

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:18.479
which took place four years ago,

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:22.369
and let's assume a similar tsunami was to take place in 2050,

00:07:22.369 --> 00:07:23.229
when the oceans

00:07:23.229 --> 00:07:25.899
are maybe

00:07:25.899 --> 00:07:27.939
even twenty five centimeters higher,

00:07:27.939 --> 00:07:31.209
which incidentally is a fairly conservative
estimate.

00:07:31.209 --> 00:07:33.500
Look at the devastation it can cause.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:35.580
If you look at the impacts on agriculture,

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.319
and this is something that agricultural scientists

00:07:38.319 --> 00:07:40.699
now have enough evidence on.

00:07:40.699 --> 00:07:42.230
They're not climate scientists.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:43.710
They don't believe in

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:46.960
anything to do with climate change, but they're seeing

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:50.509
the impacts on yields and productivity of several crops.

00:07:50.509 --> 00:07:53.339
What's that going to do to food security?

00:07:53.339 --> 00:07:57.599
Today's New York Times carries stories about
these food drives that that are taking place

00:07:57.599 --> 00:07:59.599
all over the world.

00:07:59.599 --> 00:08:01.879
That's not a result of climate change,

00:08:01.879 --> 00:08:06.439
but the point I'd like to emphasize is that
climate change only exacerbates

00:08:06.439 --> 00:08:08.810
the existing stresses on this kind,

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:10.849
whether it's water scarcity

00:08:10.849 --> 00:08:12.879
or impacts on agriculture.

00:08:12.879 --> 00:08:14.330
And a large number of

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:15.919
poor farmers

00:08:15.919 --> 00:08:19.819
in the developing countries are dependent
on rainfall agriculture.

00:08:19.819 --> 00:08:22.150
When rainfall patterns change,

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:23.459
when droughts occur

00:08:23.459 --> 00:08:26.990
on a prolonged basis, on a repeated basis,

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:28.439
what are they going to do?

00:08:28.439 --> 00:08:33.510
They're going to move to areas where they feel
they can at least live and survive.

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:35.970
Doesn't that create the danger of conflict?

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:37.550
So I think the

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:40.230
Norwegian Nobel committee

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:40.950
understood

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:43.060
the impacts of climate change

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:46.240
and how these might get worse in the future,
and therefore

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.150
pose a major threat to peace in different
parts of the world.

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:51.220
I think that's essentially the connection.

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:55.460
That is very interesting. Now, the question though is

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:59.430
large population countries like India and China

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:01.120
because of their drive to

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:03.140
grow

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:04.300
because of pressure of population

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:07.180
to give them better life,

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:10.910
they have to produce power, and their

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:13.310
most likely source is coal.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:17.580
And so the more they're going to coal-powered
electricity generation,

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:19.040
the more

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:19.880
CO2

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:20.930
is emitted.

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:23.570
So how do you square this cycle that you need

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:24.550
to have

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:26.970
growth in order to

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:29.180
reduce poverty,

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:31.100
at the same time the same growth might

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:32.540
lead to poverty

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.130
by the mechanism you mentioned.

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:37.580
Well firstly I think we must understand that
climate change

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:39.320
is being caused today

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:41.890
on account of historical factors.

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:46.280
It's in the process of industrialization, which
has taken the developed countries

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:48.810
to unprecedented levels of

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:49.750
prosperity

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:53.450
that we have emitted large quantities of
greenhouse gases,

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:57.230
and these are causing human-induced climate change today.

00:09:57.230 --> 00:10:01.500
Now, it's for this reason that the framing convention
on climate change

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:06.490
clearly lays down a common but differentiated
responsibility,

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:10.910
and that differentiated responsibility requires
the developed countries to take the first

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:11.450
steps

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:13.210
in reducing emissions.

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:16.850
Now logically what is expected is while
those countries

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:18.550
reduce their emissions,

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:22.400
the developing countries will continue
to increase their emissions,

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.430
but they don't necessarily

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:25.540
have to

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:29.000
emulate exactly what the developed countries
have done.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.520
And therefore I would say that for

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.720
local, domestic and national reasons,

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:38.130
the developing countries should certainly look at the path

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:41.900
by which they keep their emissions as low as
possible,

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:45.540
but naturally they're not going to do this
at a higher cost

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:48.810
than what existing technologies will permit.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:52.060
And today as it happens for a country that
has coal,

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:56.620
they have no choice but to burn coal to produce power.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:58.260
But what I would say is that

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:01.590
we should use coal as efficiently as
possible,

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:05.700
and we should use all the energy that's
provided to consumers, to industries

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:06.990
and to others

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:09.190
in an extremely efficient manner,

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:13.040
and to the extent possible we should also
harness other technologies

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.360
like renewable energy technologies,

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:19.350
which didn't exist a hundred years ago, but they
are available today.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:23.520
So being as developing countries have to exercise
choices

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:26.250
that will promote sustainable development.

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:29.520
We cannot possibly do this just because
the developed world

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:31.670
has followed a particular path

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:35.710
that we must do exactly what they have done because
they have made mistakes,

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:37.670
and the cost of those mistakes

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:39.330
are now being felt

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:44.110
In the most unprivileged regions of the world.

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:45.900
There have been some

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:48.680
demand that the developed countries

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:49.840
should provide

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:51.700
either financial assistance

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:53.480
or technological assistance

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:54.710
to the countries

00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:59.530
in order to reduce the CO2 emission. 
Do you think that that is

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:03.980
a possibility? Well again, that is clearly
laid down in the framework convention on climate

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:04.690
change.

00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:10.490
There I must say the developed countries have not done

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:12.400
anything substantial.

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:14.589
And if you really want to promote

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:17.920
sustainable development

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:20.319
and a climate-friendly path of development

00:12:20.319 --> 00:12:22.350
in the developing countries

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:23.480
then I think

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:25.450
the developed countries

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:27.510
must facilitate

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:29.820
the adoption of

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:31.730
clean technologies,

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:34.380
financing of some of the infrastructure, which

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:36.590
incorporates these clean technologies,

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:39.580
but that has really not been done so far.

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:42.620
And I think the demand for 

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:44.370
action in this area is now

00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:45.860
getting much louder,

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:48.420
and therefore the industrialized countries

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:51.110
will have to pay heed to it.

00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:53.580
The fear is

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:55.830
that this you do first

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:57.360
before we do,

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:00.990
this argument the west is saying
that unless you start

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:02.580
capping your emission,

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:04.070
we are not going to do it,

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:08.190
how do you resolve this dispute?

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:11.570
Well, I think it's a very unfortunate dispute
because 

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:16.760
I think it's not totally inequitable, to my
mind it's also unethical

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:19.020
for the developed countries to insist

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:22.810
that the poorest countries in the world 
should take the first steps.

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:26.000
I mean look at the reality of India.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:30.080
Yes, we are increasing our emissions, but what is 
it in per capita terms?

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:33.090
Barely one ton per capita per year.

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:40.240
And if you take the US, it's over twenty tons
per capita,

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:43.770
so you know the huge disparity that
exists today

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:45.550
really can't be wished away,

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:49.660
and I think you also have to accept the fact
that in India there are four hundred million

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:50.320
people

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:53.640
who have no access to modern energy or electricity.

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:55.990
So how can you deny them that?

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:59.329
I mean every slum-dweller in India

00:13:59.329 --> 00:14:00.920
watches television.

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:02.960
What do they see on the good life?

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:05.020
He sees the western pattern of

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:06.330
living,

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:09.440
and he feels am I not entitled to the same
thing?

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:12.450
So I think there has to be a common effort

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:14.350
in both parts of the globe

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:15.300
by which

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:18.740
the developed countries cut down their emissions rapidly,

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:22.390
and this will require much more efficient
use of energy,

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:25.350
much less dependence on fossil fuels,

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:29.140
and I would say even changes in lifestyles.

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:32.519
There has been some criticism
of the IPCC

00:14:32.519 --> 00:14:37.560
reports, saying the IPCC report grossly
underestimated

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:38.960
the

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:42.000
growth of the undeveloped countries

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.340
and secondly

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:48.030
the challenge up producing alternate energy sources.

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:50.250
How do you respond to that criticism?

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:51.700
Well, you see,

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:54.900
as I explained earlier the IPCC goes by

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:57.740
peer reviewed literature,

00:14:57.740 --> 00:15:00.630
and there's a wealth of information that is available.

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:02.680
We have to take a balanced view

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:05.030
that always some publications--

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:07.000
just to give you an example,

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:10.460
there is growing concern that sea level rise
in this would

00:15:10.460 --> 00:15:12.370
may rise to several meters,

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:13.600
and that could happen

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:16.170
if let's say the western arctic ice sheet

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:17.940
or the Greenland ice sheet

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:21.220
were to collapse. Now these are deglaciating,

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:22.580
they are melting very rapidly,

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:24.840
so that possibility exists.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:27.910
But you know the IPCC cannot take

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:32.300
an extreme view on these things because firstly
we cannot predict if it could

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:33.290
happen,

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:36.300
and let's see if it could happen when it's
going to happen.

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:39.530
So we are in no position to make that kind
of a projection,

00:15:40.740 --> 00:15:42.920
and we have to take the balance

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:46.230
of knowledge and what's contained in the literature.

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:50.540
In the case of the growth rates of the
developing countries,

00:15:50.540 --> 00:15:53.820
we have a whole range of scenarios we can assess.

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:56.670
And these are very plausible scenarios.

00:15:56.670 --> 00:15:58.470
They take into account

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:01.750
high rates of growth, high rates of technological

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:02.770
evolution,

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:06.170
high rates of population growth,

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:07.990
so we have covered

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:11.290
all the possibilities that one can foresee

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:14.229
on a reasonable basis in terms of

00:16:14.229 --> 00:16:15.760
economic growth and development

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:18.570
in the twenty first century.

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:20.770
So that criticism is not valid.

00:16:20.770 --> 00:16:25.240
We have a wide range of projections we have come up with.

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:27.270
How...in the development of 

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:29.910
new alternative technology

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:31.080
where is the

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:32.810
initiative going to come from?

00:16:32.810 --> 00:16:38.280
Because some people say there should be a 
Manhattan-like project

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:40.760
for the governments to put in

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:42.130
large sums of money

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:44.410
to promote development of alternative technology.

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:49.830
You know you really need a range of initiatives.
Firstly you've got to

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:51.670
have a policy framework

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:53.430
that promotes

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:56.529
expenditure on research and development. Yes, governments

00:16:56.529 --> 00:16:59.960
can certainly spend more money on R&amp;D.

00:16:59.960 --> 00:17:00.689
But companies

00:17:00.689 --> 00:17:03.510
and business organizations also need to do
that.

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:04.980
When will they do that?

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:07.010
When you have a price on carbon.

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:10.750
And I think it's absolutely essential if we want to move

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:13.610
towards lower emissions of carbon dioxide

00:17:13.610 --> 00:17:15.150
that we must place a

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:16.350
price on carbon.

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:17.939
If we were to do that

00:17:17.939 --> 00:17:20.020
then business, industry

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:21.390
and consumers

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:22.809
will move towards

00:17:22.809 --> 00:17:24.630
low-carbon options.

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:26.199
But we don't have that today,

00:17:26.199 --> 00:17:28.360
and I think this is where

00:17:28.360 --> 00:17:31.590
governments must see their responsibility.

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:34.679
I was very

00:17:34.679 --> 00:17:38.610
bothered to read one of the leaders
over here in this country

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:42.610
saying that we should do away with taxes
on gasoline.

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:44.890
Now to me that's a backwards step.

00:17:44.890 --> 00:17:49.309
I don't think we should indulge in these
populist measures

00:17:49.309 --> 00:17:50.769
if they're going to hurt 

00:17:50.769 --> 00:17:51.960
this country

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:53.390
and the world.

00:17:53.390 --> 00:17:56.730
And may I say that it's in the interests of
business

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:58.220
in the US

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:00.890
to move towards low-carbon technologies

00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:04.020
because that's what the future's going to
be.

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:07.020
The question of

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:09.570
keeping quiet, people are saying

00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:14.169
at least I think Jim Hansen has written a piece
for YaleGlobal in which he's 

00:18:14.169 --> 00:18:17.630
arguing

00:18:17.630 --> 00:18:18.690
there should be zero 

00:18:18.690 --> 00:18:20.020
carbon

00:18:20.020 --> 00:18:20.990
emissions

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:23.440
achieved 

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:25.470
by 2030,

00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:25.990
and

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:27.370
the level

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:32.600
of PPM should be brought down to 350
by the end of the century. That is the

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:35.070
optimum he is asking for. Do think 

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:37.530
it's feasible?

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:41.620
Well I think it's really a question

00:18:41.620 --> 00:18:42.670
of what we

00:18:42.670 --> 00:18:46.020
identify as threat.

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:48.789
If the threat is large enough, there have been

00:18:48.789 --> 00:18:53.690
occasions where the word had to fight wars,

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:56.800
and in fighting those wars they have given
up a lot,

00:18:56.800 --> 00:18:58.530
they have made some major

00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:00.200
adjustments

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:03.940
in the way the economy grows, in the way people
consume

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:05.530
goods and services.

00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:11.230
The whole issue of the tipping point is
something which involves a value judgment,

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:12.049
and I

00:19:12.049 --> 00:19:16.830
think one aspect of this issue is not given
enough attention at all,

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:18.280
and that is

00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:21.119
the aspect of different countries

00:19:21.119 --> 00:19:23.980
and their perceptions and their interests.

00:19:23.980 --> 00:19:26.690
If you talk to the president of the Maldives,

00:19:26.690 --> 00:19:28.870
as I've done on several occasions,

00:19:28.870 --> 00:19:32.050
he'll tell you that we've crossed that tipping point

00:19:32.050 --> 00:19:32.580
because

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:36.580
I remember in 1997 there was an IPCC meeting,
which was organized

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:38.049
in the Maldives,

00:19:38.049 --> 00:19:39.840
and he stood there before us

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:44.850
at the inaugural session. He said ladies and gentlemen,
the place where you're holding this meeting

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:47.280
ten years ago was full of water,

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:50.100
it was under a foot and a half of wter

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:51.640
because there was a huge

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:53.630
storm surge,

00:19:53.630 --> 00:19:56.890
which brought in large quantities of water in
this island, and

00:19:56.890 --> 00:19:58.489
most of the islands over there are

00:19:58.489 --> 00:20:01.470
a meter or a maximum of two meters high.

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:03.940
So if you talk to the small island states,

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:08.190
if you talk to people in coastal areas or
those in sub-Saharan Africa,

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.980
they'll tell you that the tipping point has been crossed.

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:14.730
Now the question is when you come up with the global response,

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:18.520
whose concerns are you going to take into account?

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:20.110
Gandhi had a philosophy 

00:20:20.110 --> 00:20:21.780
where he

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:23.240
talked about "until death",

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:25.539
which means

00:20:25.539 --> 00:20:26.539
the impact

00:20:26.539 --> 00:20:30.470
on the most underprivileged, the last man.

00:20:30.470 --> 00:20:31.529
I think if

00:20:31.529 --> 00:20:33.760
world is taking a decision

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:35.330
on what needs to be done,

00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:37.340
I think we should keep in mind

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:39.670
the most...

00:20:39.670 --> 00:20:41.440
the worst affected

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:44.030
nations or societies in the world

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:46.320
in defining what the tipping point is.

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:48.130
I don't think we're doing that.

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:50.580
We're only concerned about ourselves,

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:53.600
and since this is a very unequal worlD,

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:56.450
unfortunately it is the prosperous who seem to lay down

00:20:56.450 --> 00:20:59.550
the requirements of what needs to be done

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:00.170
and

00:21:00.170 --> 00:21:01.680
what is not to be done.

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:04.820
Precisely because of that concern, a
lot of people

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:09.330
fear that the Copenhagen Process, the next
step of Kyoto 2,

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:12.560
might be taken

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:15.570
in a direction by the developed countries,

00:21:15.570 --> 00:21:16.590
which will again

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:19.210
serve their interests and not the interests of the planet.

00:21:19.210 --> 00:21:21.850
What do you see is the prospect of
Copenhagen?

00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:26.220
Well I personally feel there is a major change that is
taking place, and I

00:21:26.220 --> 00:21:31.169
don't think as chairman of the IPCC,
I feel the IPCC should

00:21:31.169 --> 00:21:32.419
get the credit,

00:21:32.419 --> 00:21:33.840
but certainly

00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:36.540
the impact our reports have had last year

00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:38.230
has been astounding,

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:42.140
and I think it will change  public opinion
all over the world.

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:42.900
Therefore

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:44.110
my

00:21:44.110 --> 00:21:46.020
hope is that

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:50.050
since the public is now so concerned about
the impact not only in other parts of the

00:21:50.050 --> 00:21:50.790
world,

00:21:50.790 --> 00:21:53.630
but even in this country,

00:21:53.630 --> 00:21:56.730
that there will be a strong resolve and
a desire

00:21:56.730 --> 00:21:59.230
to take the right steps.

00:21:59.230 --> 00:22:02.730
Also we can't wish away the fact that the

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:04.460
developing countries

00:22:04.460 --> 00:22:06.350
including the emerging markets

00:22:06.350 --> 00:22:09.590
are no longer lightweights who can be bought,

00:22:09.590 --> 00:22:12.010
they have a voice in global affairs.

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:13.549
And therefore I think

00:22:13.549 --> 00:22:16.740
their perceptions will have to be taken into
account.

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:19.760
So one hopes that there will be a fair

00:22:19.760 --> 00:22:23.669
and equitable agreement that we come up
with in Copenhagen, but

00:22:23.669 --> 00:22:28.200
I do realize there are several pitfalls and
problems along the way.

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:29.650
Well, on that somewhat

00:22:29.650 --> 00:22:31.290
positive note,

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:32.820
thank you very much for all your time.

